-- title:
--   fpqc-local lifting for a formally étale uniformisation
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $r$ and a discrete valuation domain $\mathcal O$ with an irreducible element $\pi$ such that $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and $(r)=(\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$, and let $K_0$ be a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Let $Onr$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra which is a domain of characteristic zero in which the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ is maximal and each element satisfies some monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ whose value at that element lies in that ideal. Let $G$ be a type and let $fM : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ be a morphism of schemes that is locally of finite type. For each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which the image of $\pi$ is nilpotent, write $M(B)$ for the set of morphisms $\operatorname{Spec} B \to M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and let $\Theta f$ be given maps $\bigl((Onr \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}_{K_0,\pi}(B)\bigr) \times G \to M(B)$, where a Deligne datum over $B$ assigns to every full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M$ in $K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, compatibly with lattice inclusions, equivariantly for homotheties by $K_0^\times$, and satisfying the nondegeneracy condition of the definition at every prime of $B$. Three hypotheses on $\Theta f$ are assumed: naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; formal étaleness, namely that for every surjection $p : B \to B_0$ of such algebras whose kernel has square zero, every $x_0$ over $B_0$ and every $y \in M(B)$ with $p_*y = \Theta f(x_0)$ admit a unique $x$ over $B$ with $p_*x = x_0$ and $\Theta f(x)=y$; and surjectivity on geometric points, namely that for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-field $k$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, every $\psi : Onr \to_{\mathcal O} k$ and every $y \in M(k)$ there are $P$ and $g \in G$ with $\Theta f((\psi,P),g) = y$. The conclusion is that for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $y \in M(B)$ there are finitely many $B$-algebras $B'_1,\dots,B'_k$, each an $\mathcal O$-algebra compatibly with its $B$-algebra structure, each flat as a $B$-module, such that every prime of $B$ is the contraction of a prime of some $B'_i$, $\pi$ is nilpotent in each $B'_i$, and there are points $x'_i$ over $B'_i$ with $\Theta f(x'_i)$ equal to the image of $y$ in $M(B'_i)$.
--
--   This is the fpqc-local lifting step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation argument: a formally étale family of maps from the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$, Drinfeld's formal upper half plane functor and a constant set into the nilpotent points of $M$, surjective on geometric points, admits lifts after a finite flat covering of each base. It is used in the proof that such a uniformisation factors uniquely through a fine moduli family, via [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuliT.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuliT.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine).
